Dynamic Ontology Derivation

	Philosophy

		-
			Dynamic ontology derivation is a process wherein an AI agent is given a subject to
			research online, during which it is to persist any relevant information, called
			ontological evidence, to a database in whatever structure suits it. Following this, it
			is to derive from this data a standard ontology and to persist said ontology to a
			separate database. This process may be repeated such that the unstructured data may be
			continuously updated and the corresponding ontology may be updated in turn to reflect
			it.
		-

	Principles

		Standard Ontology

			-
				A standard ontology consists of a set of discrete objects, called standard ontology
				objects, which shall each have a string type, name or ID, and value, and which may
				have an indefinite number of relationships, with each relationship having a source
				object, a target object, a string type, and a flag indicating whether it is
				unidirectional or bidirectional.

				The set of types used by the objects of a given standard ontology, together with
				the set of types used by the relationships of said objects, constitute the
				ontological schema of the ontology.
			-

		Portal Derivation

			-
				Portal Derivation is where a UI is constructed specifically to fit a standard
				ontology.
			-

		Dynamic Deriver

			-
				A dynamic deriver is an agent specifically designed to conduct dynamic ontology
				derivation, with the creation of a standard ontology from the gathered evidence,
				followed by portal derivation for said ontology.

				A dynamic deriver may fit the generated ontology to a user specified ontological
				schema, or to an extended version thereof.
			-